"Before Retrieval Begins: Safety and Tool-Use Decisions in LLM Agents Warning: This paper contains model-generated content related to harmful behaviors, included for research purposes. (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23071v1)\nciteturn41308view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23071v1\",\"lineno\":186}); Total lines: 360\nL171: We next ask whether this relationship extends beyond behavioral disagreement. We estimate a text-refusal direction $d_{\\text{text}}$ without the tool. It is anti-aligned with $d_{c}$ in every model (Figure cite89†4 ). At exploratory doses selected separately for each model, steering $d_{\\text{text}}$ changes admission relative to a matched random direction in four models (Table cite88†3 ). The effect ranges from a 71.4-point reduction to an 11.9-point increase.\nL172: The intervention reveals a model-dependent relationship between text refusal and admission.\nL173: Figure 4: Signed alignment between the text-refusal direction $d_{\\text{text}}$ and the admission-outcome direction $d_{c}$ across normalized depth. Diamonds mark operation-probe-selected layers; faint lines show each model’s maximum absolute cosine over 10 random controls.\nL174: Model Ref.–call (%) $d_{\\text{text}}$ excess [95% CI] Control Llama-3.2-3B 23.7 $0.0$ [$0.0$, $0.0$] saturated Llama-3.1-8B 11.5 $-5.8$ [$-7.7$, $-3.9$] valid Granite-3.1-8B 27.5 $+0.2$ [$0.0$, $+0.5$] saturated Mistral-7B 0.0 $0.0$ [$0.0$, $0.0$] floor Hermes-3-8B 44.0 $-33.3$ [$-37.6$, $-28.9$] valid Mistral-Nemo 46.6 $-71.4$ [$-75.9$, $-66.8$] valid Qwen2.5-7B 43.2 $+11.9$ [$+8.8$, $+15.0$] valid 7-model total 33.6 — —\nL175: Table 3: Behavioral refusal–call divergence (%) and dose-matched text-refusal steering excess (pp).\nL176: \nL177: Our final test changes the training signal rather than the hidden state. We fine-tune each model on 80 harmful behaviors paired with plain-text refusals and 80 benign instruction–response pairs. The training behaviors are disjoint from the 296 test behaviors, and training contains no tool calls. We use one fixed recipe and one seed per model (Appendix cite28†A.6 ).\nL178: Plain-text refusal training lowers harmful admission by 19.6 points in Llama-3.1-8B, 54.8 in Hermes-3, and 61.5 in Mistral-Nemo-12B (Figure cite90†5 ; Table cite87†19 ). These models retain 94.9–99.0% benign calling. Under this single-seed pilot, these three model–recipe combinations provide evidence that text-refusal training can sometimes transfer to admission while preserving the tested benign behavior.\nL179: The same recipe does not transfer cleanly to every model. Llama-3.2-3B reduces harmful admission but also reduces benign calling to 80.7%. Qwen2.5-7B produces repeated tool-call tokens, while Granite-3.1-8B and Mistral-7B stop calling on benign requests. These runs do not isolate refusal transfer and are reported in Appendix cite28†A.6 . The successful and inconclusive runs together show that text-refusal training does not provide a reliable control over admission.\nL180: For users and deployers, written refusal cannot certify that the call was withheld. Future work should directly compare refusal-only, admission-only, and joint supervision under matched training budgets and broader legitimate tool-use evaluations.\nL181: Figure 5: Single-seed plain-text refusal fine-tuning pilot: harmful request admission rate (%) before and after fine-tuning on disjoint behaviors. Three model–recipe combinations preserve 95–99% benign calling; Llama-3.2-3B falls to 80.7%.\nL182: ## 5 Conclusion\nL183: Within our generated construction, the requested-operation component produces the largest tool-admission contrast and admission is only partially coupled to written refusal. The requested operation changes admission by 19 percentage points, and its contrast is anti-aligned with the admission outcome across all seven open-weight models. Removing this direction influences admission on unseen behaviors in four models.\nL184: Its effects on operation-neutralized requests show that the influence is not specific to harmful-operation semantics. Written refusal accompanies 33.6% of admitted harmful calls, and representation steering produces heterogeneous changes in admission. Under one single-seed recipe, plain-text refusal training reduces harmful admission in three model–recipe combinations that retain benign tool use; the remaining runs do not support a clean transfer conclusion.\nL185: The fixed-source design makes these request and representation contrasts comparable, and downstream scoring shows why admission matters: the unweighted model-average harm rate after admitted harmful requests is 74.5%. These findings identify tool admission as a distinct safety control point. For users, a refusal displayed in the final response does not establish that the agent withheld the call.\nL186: Future work should compare direct admission supervision with refusal-only and joint training, test whether control transfers across retrieval and state-changing tools, and evaluate broader legitimate tool use.\nL187: ## 6 Limitations\nL188: Our conclusions concern tool admission for a web-retrieval tool that fetches one externally supplied URL. This design isolates tool admission from query formulation, ranking, and source selection. Its scope excludes agents that choose among tools or execute state-changing actions. A call can also be appropriate for classification or moderation, even when the request is harmful.\nL189: We therefore measure harmful responses after retrieval and leave the direct risks of messaging, payment, and code-execution tools to future work. Our benign control covers URL summarization; broader forms of benign tool use may yield different utility trade-offs.\nL190: The primary experiments cover 296 English behaviors and seven open-weight models from 3B to 12B parameters. Three closed models reproduce the behavioral admission contrast and component ordering, while the mechanistic analyses require open weights. The reported rates may change for larger models, other languages, native tool-use systems, and agents with proprietary routing or policy layers. The factorial analysis further covers 147 behaviors with separable request components.\nL191: Its generated ablations alter functional meaning and sometimes actionability. Consequently, the 19 pp operation effect measures sensitivity to the requested-operation component as constructed; it cannot isolate harmfulness or distinguish semantic sensitivity from lexical sensitivity.\nL192: The held-out intervention exceeds its random control in four of seven models and varies across folds, supporting a model-dependent linear influence. Its effect on operation-neutralized requests also indicates a broad influence on calling. The cross-model geometry and neuron-localization results remain descriptive. Monitor performance comes from grouped cross-validation on controlled prompts and synthetic topic-matched benign requests, so deployment under natural distribution shift remains untested.\nL193: The fine-tuning study uses one recipe and one seed per model, and only three models preserve sufficient benign tool use for the coupling comparison. We therefore treat it as pilot evidence of possible transfer, not a general training result. Finally, a fixed GPT-4o judge scores downstream harm. Human, Claude, and Gemini validation provide substantial agreement, but automated judgments can still contain systematic errors.\nL194: ## 7 Ethics Statement\nL195: This work studies a dual-use safety problem. Knowledge of tool admission can support safeguards, but it could also inform attempts to elicit unsafe tool use. We limit the study to established HarmBench-derived behaviors and cached retrieval sources (cite49†Mazeika et al., 2024 ; cite44†Nawal et al., 2026 ). The experiments introduce no new harmful task categories or jailbreak procedures.\nL196: They use a read-only tool with cached content, perform all representation interventions offline, and execute no live browsing or state-changing actions. The paper reports aggregate results and omits step-by-step harmful completions.\nL197: The study collects no personal data. One research-group annotator evaluated 400 model outputs while blind to model identity and condition. Because this task can expose annotators and researchers to harmful material, raw prompts, pages, and outputs require controlled access and appropriate researcher support. The proposed monitors may also block benign requests concerning sensitive topics.\nL198: Any deployment should therefore measure false positives on domain-specific traffic and apply review, logging, and escalation procedures that match the consequences of the tool.\nL199: ## References\nL200:   * Andriushchenko et al. (2025) M. Andriushchenko, A. Souly, M. Dziemian, D. Duenas, M. Lin, J. Wang, D. Hendrycks, A. Zou, Z. Kolter, M. Fredrikson, E. Winsor, J. Wynne, Y. Gal, and X. Davies AgentHarm: a benchmark for measuring harmfulness of LLM agents. External Links: 2410.09024, cite91†Link Cited by: cite92†§2 .\nL201:   * Arditi et al. (2024) A. Arditi, O. Obeso, A. Syed, D. Paleka, N. Panickssery, W. Gurnee, and N. Nanda Refusal in language models is mediated by a single direction. External Links: 2406.11717, cite93†Link Cited by: cite94†§2 , cite95†§3.4 .\nL202:   * Bai et al. (2022) Y. Bai, S. Kadavath, S. Kundu, A. Askell, J. Kernion, A. Jones, A. Chen, A. Goldie, A. Mirhoseini, C. McKinnon, et al. Constitutional AI: harmlessness from AI feedback. arXiv preprint arXiv:2212.08073. Cited by: cite96†§1 .\nL203:   * Cartagena and Teixeira (2026) A. Cartagena and A. Teixeira Mind the GAP: text safety does not transfer to tool-call safety in LLM agents. External Links: 2602.16943, cite97†Link Cited by: cite98†§1 , cite99†§1 , cite94†§2 , cite100†§4.4 .\nL204:   * Chen et al. (2026) Y. Chen, V. Siu, Y. Liu, D. Song, 