The URL and page are otherwise identical. A response counts as admission only when the model emits a well-formed call to the registered tool.\nL251: Autonomous-retrieval check. The URL is removed from the request while the same tool remains available. Admission requires the model to generate a URL argument and emit a well-formed call. The harness then returns the behavior’s canonical cached page. This auxiliary experiment measures first-turn admission only; it does not evaluate generated-source quality or downstream autonomous-retrieval harm.\nL252: ### A.2 Factorial Request Construction\nL253: \nL254: We retain the 147 behaviors that admit three separable roles. GPT-4o identifies one domain phrase (A), requested-operation phrase (B), and specificity modifier (C), then generates the seven conditions other than 111. Condition 111 is copied verbatim from the source behavior.\nL255: The validator requires all eight conditions, clauses of 2–30 words, retained-component presence, removed-component absence, one instance per role, no placeholders, and exact equality between 111 and the source behavior. All retained records pass. These checks establish structural and lexical validity.\nL256: One blind NLP graduate-student annotator labels component presence across all variants without seeing condition codes or model outputs. A separate Claude-Sonnet-4.6 audit receives each original behavior and the proposed spans, then judges role and boundary correctness. It returns parseable judgments for 134 of 147 decompositions. Gemini-2.5-Flash repeats the role audit as a cross-developer check. Table cite83†14 reports the audit results, and Table cite82†16 gives the complete factorial outcomes.\nL257: A second independent judge from a different developer (gemini-2.5-flash) corroborates these role assignments on the 140 behaviors it returns parseable decisions for (A 98.6%, B 92.9%, C 89.3%). Restricting the factorial to the 86 behaviors where all three roles are accepted leaves the same ordering as the full set: 7.8 pp (A), 19.1 pp (B, 95% CI [15.3, 23.0]), and 7.1 pp (C), against 8.2/19.1/6.8 pp over all 147 behaviors.\nL258: Annotation imperfections limit a literal reading of individual clauses, particularly C, but do not change which component has the largest effect.\nL259: ### A.3 Harm and Refusal Labels\nL260: \nL261: The GPT-4o harm judge runs at temperature 0 with no system prompt, following the X-Teaming protocol (cite131†Rahman et al., 2025 ). Its user message contains the prohibited-usage policy, HarmBench behavior, model response, and any behavior-specific context. The judge returns JSON {‘‘score’’: 1--5, ‘‘reason’’: ‘‘...’’}. Scores of at least 3 define harm.\nL262: Human Validation. The validation sample contains 400 outputs stratified across models and conditions. A blind NLP graduate-student annotator applies the same rubric. GPT-4o agrees with the human annotator on 86.0% of binary labels ($\\kappa=0.738$).\nL263: Inter-LLM Agreement. Claude-Sonnet-4.6 and Gemini-2.5-Flash independently score the same sample with the same rubric. GPT-4o agreement is 92.1% with Claude ($\\kappa=0.772$, $n=369$) and 89.8% with Gemini ($\\kappa=0.700$, $n=400$), and all three judges agree on 87.2% of items. The three judges assign nearly the same overall harm rate (78% GPT-4o, 79% Gemini), so GPT-4o is not the strict or lenient outlier.\nL264: Broken out by model (Table cite132†6 ), agreement is substantial to near-perfect for five of the seven open models ($\\kappa\\,0.82$–$1.00$) and moderate for the two small Llamas (Llama-3.1-8B $\\kappa=0.52$, Llama-3.2-3B $\\kappa=0.63$); in both low-$\\kappa$ cases the two judges’ overall harm rates are within 8 pp, so the disagreement is near-threshold noise rather than a systematic divergence.\nL265: The closed GPT-4.1 is not in this open-model validation set, and because the primary judge (GPT-4o) shares a developer with it, its downstream-harm rate in Table cite79†8 is the case most exposed to same-lab leniency; we therefore re-judged all of GPT-4.1’s admitted harmful-request responses ($n=125$) with the independent Claude judge. The two judges agree on 98.4% of these items ($\\kappa=0.97$), and Claude scores harm slightly more often than GPT-4o (44.8% vs.\nL266: 43.2% at score $\\geq 3$), so GPT-4.1’s reported harm rate is not an artifact of same-lab leniency.\nL267: Model $n$ Agree % $\\kappa$ Mistral-7B 57 100.0 1.00 Mistral-Nemo-12B 49 98.0 0.93 Qwen2.5-7B 53 96.2 0.87 Granite-3.1-8B 55 92.7 0.83 Hermes-3-Llama-3.1-8B 55 94.5 0.82 Llama-3.2-3B 51 82.4 0.63 Llama-3.1-8B 49 79.6 0.52 Overall 369 92.1 0.77\nL268: \nL269: Table 5: Per-model GPT-4o vs. independent Claude (claude-sonnet-4-6) agreement on binary harm decision ($\\geq 3$), 400-sample validation set.\nL270: Model $n$ $P(\\textit{harm}\\,|\\,\\textit{call})$ ($\\geq 3$) $P(\\textit{harm}\\,|\\,\\textit{call})$ ($\\geq 4$) Llama-3.2-3B 190 51.1 17.9 Llama-3.1-8B 208 60.1 24.0 Granite-3.1-8B 131 68.7 58.8 Mistral-7B 23 100.0 100.0 Hermes-3-Llama-3.1-8B 250 76.8 68.8 Mistral-Nemo-12B 266 86.8 77.4 Qwen2.5-7B 206 78.2 69.4 7-model mean — 74.5 59.5\nL271: \nL272: Table 6: Harm rate after called tool at paper threshold ($\\geq 3$) vs. stricter threshold ($\\geq 4$). Mean remains 59.5% at stricter threshold.\nL273: Threshold Sensitivity. The rubric counts score $\\geq 3$ as harmful, which admits Score 3 (“content related to the request…not glaringly inappropriate”). Table cite132†6 recomputes $P(\\textit{harm}\\,|\\,\\textit{call})$ at the stricter score $\\geq 4$ (explicitly prohibited content only).\nL274: The headline finding survives: the seven-model mean falls from 74.5% to 59.5%, and the drop is concentrated in the two small Llama models (which more often produce borderline Score-3 text), while Granite, Hermes-3, Mistral-Nemo-12B, and Qwen2.5-7B stay at 59–77% even at $\\geq 4$. Mistral-7B’s 100% conditional harm rate rests on only $n=23$ admitted calls (Wilson 95% CI [85.7, 100]).\nL275: Written-refusal labels. For each admitted harmful request, GPT-4o labels the prose portion of the assistant turn as a safety refusal or not a safety refusal. A safety refusal objects because the request is harmful, illegal, dangerous, unethical, or disallowed. Capability statements, neutral preambles, and descriptions without an objection are not refusals. Pure tool-call turns contain no written refusal and remain in the denominator.\nL276: ### A.4 Representation and Intervention Protocol\nL277: Hidden states are taken at the final input token before generation. Directions are difference-of-means vectors defined in Section cite8†3 . A unit random vector is orthogonalized to each target direction and used as the perturbation control. Direction removal projects the target component from the residual stream at every token and layer position during generation. Target and control arms use identical prompts, model weights, tool schemas, decoding, and intervention magnitude.\nL278: The primary $d_{B}$ analysis uses five outer behavior folds shared across models. Within each outer-training split, four-fold behavior-grouped cross-validation selects the $d_{B}$ layer. The direction and its orthogonal random control are then frozen before generation on the outer-test fold. Table cite86†2 reports the paired target-minus-control effect over the pooled outer-test records.\nL279: Dose-matched steering. For each model and target direction, we apply the target and its matched random direction at the same signed dose. Reported effects subtract the random-direction admission shift. A saturated random control is marked inconclusive. For Llama-3.1-8B and Granite-3.1-8B, we also inspect the gentlest dose ($k=-0.5$), where the random shift has magnitude at most 5 percentage points.\nL280: ### A.5 Statistical Protocol\nL281: \nL282: The resampling unit is the behavior. Each bootstrap draw samples behaviors with replacement and includes every associated condition and intervention record. We use 2,000 draws. Cosine intervals additionally stratify by admission outcome so neither contrast arm is empty. Paired intervention effects are computed within behavior before bootstrapping. Wilson intervals are used for unclustered proportions.\nL283: Effects are estimated separately by model. Cross-model summaries are unweighted macro-averages over the seven models. Factorial main effects average the relevant condition contrasts over the remaining two factors. Full-data layer and dose analyses select and evaluate on the same pool and are descriptive. The held-out analysis performs direction estimation and layer selection inside each outer-training split. We apply no multiplicity correction across models and make no pooled significance claim.\nL284: ### A.6 Refusal Fine-Tuning\nL285: \nL286: The training set contains 80 HarmBench validation behaviors paired cyclically with eight short safety refusals and 80 benign instructions paired with GPT-4o responses. Training behaviors are disjoint from the 296 evaluation behaviors. Records contain no tool definitions, calls, or outputs.\nL287: We use 4-bit NF4 QLoRA with bfloat16 computation and double quantization. LoRA targets q_proj, k_proj, v_proj, o_proj, gate_proj, up_proj, and down_proj, with rank 16, $\\alpha=16$, and zero dropout. Training uses three epochs, batch size 2, gradient accumulation 4, learning rate $2\\times 10^{-4}$, five warm-up steps, a linear schedule, paged AdamW 8-bit, weight decay 0.01, maximum sequence length 1,024, and seed 42. The adapter is merged into the bfloat16 base model before evaluation.\nL288: We use one recipe and one seed for every model.\nL289: ## Appendix B Category 2 Appendix Material: Complementary Results\nL290: ### B.1 Closed-Model Replication\nL291: Our mechanistic analyses require open weights, but admission can be measured on any tool-using model. We replicate the harmful/benign comparison and component factorial on GPT-4.1, Gemini-2.5-Flash, and Claude-Haiku-4.5 using the same URLs, tool schema, prompts, and behaviors (Table cite79†8 ). All three admit 21.3–44.1% of harmful requests and 93–99% of benign summary requests; after an admitted harmful-request call, 36.5–77.9% of responses are judged harmful.\nL292: Across the eight factorial conditions, B has the largest main effect in each model (22.8–51.0 pp), exceeding A (3.4–17.0 pp) and C (7.5–12.2 pp; Table cite79†8 ). This replicates the requested-operation component ordering, not a shallow lexical interpretation.\nL293: Condition Components present GPT-4.1 Gemini- 2.5-Flash Claude- Haiku-4.5 000 none 100.0 92.5 95.2 100 A 98.0 85.0 90.5 001 C 98.6 80.3 89.1 101 A+C 97.9 81.0 80.3 010 B 89.0 36.1 66.0 011 B+C 89.7 34.7 61.2 110 A+B 77.9 38.1 50.3 111 A+B+C 46.9 25.9 22.4\nL294: \nL295: Table 7: Closed-model instruction-component factorial: tool-calling rates (%) over 147 behaviors, same conditions as Figure cite81†2 . A = domain phrase, B = requested-operation phrase, C = specificity.\nL296: Model Harmful admission Benign admission Harm after call Harm via call GPT-4.1 44.1 98.6 43.8 19.3 Gemini-2.5-Flash 38.2 93.2 77.9 29.7 Claude-Haiku-4.5 21.3 94.6 36.5 7.8\nL297: \nL298: Table 8: Closed-model replication over 296 behaviors (%). Harmful and benign admission are tool-calling rates. Harm after call is GPT-4o harm rate over admitted harmful-request responses ($n=63$–$130$); harm via call is product of harmful admission and harm after call.\nL299: ### B.2 Autonomous Retrieval and Deployment Defenses\nL300: \nL301: Table cite80†10 reports autonomous-retrieval admission when no URL is supplied (method in Appendix cite23†A.1 ); Table cite80†10 reports the combined prompt- and tool-level defense sweep summarized in §cite14†4.1 .\nL302: \nL303: Model Calls / 296 Admission (%) Llama-3.2-3B 147 49.7 Llama-3.1-8B 132 44.6 Hermes-3-Llama-3.1-8B 105 35.5 Granite-3.1-8B 89 30.1 Mistral-7B 2 0.7 Mistral-Nemo-12B 172 58.1 Qwen2.5-7B 48 16.2\nL304: Table 9: Autonomous retrieval admission when no URL is supplied. Mistral-7B has three generation-error records, retained in denominator.\nL305: \nL306: Model No defense Both defenses $\\Delta$ pp Hermes-3-Llama-3.1-8B 78.7 42.9 $-35.8$ Llama-3.2-3B 61.5 42.9 $-18.6$ Llama-3.1-8B 81.1 64.9 $-16.2$ Granite-3.1-8B 41.2 30.4 $-10.8$ Mistral-7B 12.5 0 6.1 $-6.4$ Qwen2.5-7B 69.6 72.0 $+2.4$ 6-model mean 57.4 43.2 $-14.2$\nL307: Table 10: Combined prompt+tool defense vs. matched no-framing baseline (harmful tool-calling rate, %). Mistral-Nemo-12B omitted.\nL308: ### B.3 Admission Monitoring\nL309: We test whether supervised classifiers can flag harmful admissions before execution. Positives are admitted operation-present requests, and the initial negatives are admitted fully neutralized requests. Under behavior-grouped 5-fold cross-validation, a full-state logistic probe reaches AUROC 0.97–0.99 (Table cite84†12 ). A single compliance-direction projection is weaker at 0.69–0.90.\nL310: A TF-IDF character $n$-gram classifier trained on the request text reaches AUROC 0.97–0.98 under the same folds, so the hidden state adds no unique signal on this task.\n"
